tcome":"PASS"},{"evidence":"sweep-records.test.js:109-143. Both round-4 paired packets carry format, guide and [{findingId, classId:'swept-class', round:4, ...sweep}], and roundPolicy.revision matches sha256. Round 5 without claims carries the same single record (both peers deduplicated). The round-6 individual gate.execute carries it plus a second record for the re-claim. scopeSweepRecords returns 2 records for an open scope and [] when reviewScopes has no entry. :145-152 shows a dropped stale claim gives no sweepRecords and packet.claims [], with roundPolicy.revision present. :154-157 checks content identity for duplicates. Implementation: sweep-records.js:69-109, execution.js:991-1000,1100.","id":"R3","outcome":"PASS"},{"evidence":"sweep-records.test.js:159-182 runs for rounds 4 and 5. A new medium missed-site of swept-class on a path the fix did not change gives FAIL. A medium finding of another class is demoted (PASS, one demoted). An earlier swept-class finding F1 still present gives FAIL. The claimed F0 still present with F1 resolved gives PASS. :184-192 checks the predicate, including later-round and null-round records. Implementation: boundary-review.js:42-47, sweep-records.js:114-117. Receipt 0 lists all 8 round cases passing.","id":"R4","outcome":"PASS"},{"evidence":"execution.js:286 contains 'When packet.sweepRecords is present, follow packet.sweepRecords.guide.', and :287 names missed sites of a class in packet.sweepRecords in the rounds 4-5 band. SWEEP_GUIDE (sweep-records.js:22) says to verify the records, report a missed site with the record's classId, and treat such a finding as a regression of the previous fix, where in rounds 4-5 a medium or high one blocks. Tested at sweep-records.test.js:194-201.","id":"R5","outcome":"PASS"},{"evidence":"round-policy.test.js:504-548 has five #134 variants: codex re-reports and claude resolves, the reverse order, unsupported, and individual reviews in both orders (status round 4 asserted). Each round-5 pair is FAIL. Receipt 0 shows these and all pre-existing round-policy tests passing. The separate CR-1 interaction (same-revision dispute re-review) is a code defect outside R6's stated test conditions.","id":"R6","outcome":"PASS"},{"evidence":"PROTOCOL.md:696-729 'Fix claims and sweep records' covers the disposition values, a sweep record example and bounds, refusal before reviewers start, and packet.sweepRecords shape and carrying rules. PROTOCOL.md:325-338 covers the swept-class regression rule and (scope, round, revision) grouping. commands/pr-review.md:23-31 explains how to write fixed (with a sweep) and disputed claims. Receipt 4 `diff -r plugin-src/shared/resources/protocol apps/desktop/resources/protocol` exited 0 with empty output, and desktop-projection.json lists current/sweep-records.js.","id":"R7","outcome":"PASS"}],"dimensions":[{"evidence":"One blocking defect, CR-1: review-findings.js:47 and :53 together with round-policy.js:26 and :61 mean a finding raised on a revision cannot be closed by a later same-revision unsupported/resolved. It surfaces in the next round as an unreconciled finding and an UNAVAILABLE pair. Two low findings: CR-2 (execution.js:865-867 keeps a stale origin when a finding ID is reused) and CR-3 (evidence). Otherwise sound: claim validation runs before launch at both entry points; claimRecords take classId from retained history (the implementer cannot relabel); the claimed finding is excluded from its own class regression; null-round records are excluded; the reuse key includes sweepRecords; blocking flags are never rewritten.","name":"code","outcome":"FAIL"},{"evidence":"AC1-AC7 are each implemented and R1-R7 each pass with test or document evidence (see criteria). The project rules from spec DoD hold: the receipts show the CLI suite subsets passing, the mirror is byte-identical, and the full CLI suite is planned in hosted CI. Material decisions D1-D7/S5-S8 match the code: required disposition, classId from the finding, records from inputs of the open scope, S7's conservative two-claim rule (sweptClassRegression compares findingId only), and S6's early refusal in review.execute. CR-1 departs from AC6's wording (it counts first raises, not only 're-report of an earlier finding ID') in a scenario the rubric does not test, so it is scored as a code defect.","name":"requirements","outcome":"PASS"},{"evidence":"Consumed Verification (evidence/1d5d8a4…; runner 5ce44ca4…) ran 5 commands on source 850684dd, all exit 0. Focused: sweep-records and round-policy (52 tests pass, stdout fffda0db…). Affected suites: boundary-review, core, reviewer-contract, verdict-normalization, history-assessment, ci-after-convergence, autonomous, and the delivery/goal/guard/light/delegation/workspace/parity/waivers/performance/robustness drivers. Also plugin validate and the mirror diff. These cover every changed protocol module and every CLI test driving claims. cli/src/commands/current.js changed help text only. The desktop suite has no local receipt (low, CR-3). ciRequiredChecks ['Ubuntu 22.04 acceptance','Ubuntu 24.04 acceptance','Desktop contract on Ubuntu 24.04'] map to plugin-ci.yml:94-112 (ci/portable-acceptance.sh: full `npm test --prefix cli`, including the host-dependent files excluded locally, python suites and plugin validate/build) and :142-160 (desktop npm test). That plan covers the approved scope, and the implementer's local alternative is adequate for a local round.","name":"evidence","outcome":"PASS"},{"evidence":"Changes are confined to the new sweep-records.js; small call sites in execution.js and boundary-review.js; the #134 fix in review-findings.js (planned) plus the S5 execution.js re-report change; the CLI help text; PROTOCOL.md; pr-review.md; the desktop restage and projection; and tests and fixtures. Planning reviews and metrics are untouched per D7. No unrelated files or local-agent-adapters.js edits were found.","name":"scope","outcome":"PASS"},{"evidence":"There is no prior history and no dispositions are required. New findings: CR-1 (medium, blocking in round 1 under the all-non-low band); CR-2 and CR-3 (low, non-blocking). The outcome is FAIL because CR-1 blocks.","name":"findings","outcome":"FAIL"}],"dispositions":[],"findings":[{"blocking":true,"classId":"round-grouping-first-raise-counted-present","classStatement":"The peer prepass counts a review's first raise of a finding as 'present in its round', so a later same-round resolution can never close it once rounds span several executions of one revision.","criterion":"gate:review","evidence":"review-findings.js:47 sets key = `round:${policy.scope}:${policy.round}:${policy.revision}` for any input with a revision, so separate review.execute and gate.execute runs of one revision share a key. round-policy.js:26 and :61 do not count a re-review of the current revision again, so roundPolicy.round is the same. review-findings.js:53 `for (const finding of report.findings ?? []) present.add(finding.id)` adds first raises, not only re-reports of earlier IDs as AC6 states ('a review's re-report of an earlier finding ID'). review-findings.js:76 then skips resolved/unsupported. The unreconciled filter at review-findings.js:88-90 accepts only resolved/unsupported/present/present-low, not unaffected. execution.js:646-650 lets a PASS carry a reconsider ID without a disposition. Base review-findings.js:39 keyed by input.reviewRound, which differs per execution. No test covers a same-revision dispute re-review followed by a new revision.","failureScenario":"Codex raises blocking finding F on revision R (round N). The implementer sends a disputed claim and re-runs review.execute on the unchanged revision R, which keeps the same round because countRound/currentRevision do not count it again. Both reviewers mark F unsupported, and this round PASSes. The next revision's review receives F as a reconsider closure: its history holds the raising report and the unsupported closure, and the fresh reviewer validly carries F as unaffected or omits it. findingObligations then keys all round-N reports of R as round:scope:N:R, so presentInRound contains F from the raising report and both unsupported dispositions are skipped. F stays open and unreconciled, and the pair is qualified as UNAVAILABLE ('Prior withheld findings require follow-up reconciliation') even though both reviewers accepted the rebuttal. Base grouped by reviewRound, so the closure applied.","id":"CR-1","path":"repository/plugin-src/shared/resources/protocol/current/review-findings.js","severity":"medium"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"reused-finding-id-origin","classStatement":"Keeping the first history reference on re-report treats a reused finding ID as the old finding, so a new finding inherits a stale origin.","criterion":"gate:review","evidence":"execution.js:865-867 (`if (known) Object.assign(known, { open: true, closure: null, reopened: ref })`) keeps the first ref. Base execution.js:867 replaced it with the re-reporting ref. review-findings.js:71 keeps known.origin unless a resolution was seen. sweep-records.js:77 uses the first history finding with the ID. Finding IDs are reviewer-chosen and checkVerdict (execution.js:597) only rejects duplicates within one verdict. Requires ID reuse plus a missing closure report.","failureScenario":"A provider's finding F1 is raised in round 1 and resolved in round 2. Its reports are left out of the round-4 packet, so the round-4 reviewer reuses ID F1 for a distinct medium regression on a path the round-4 fix changed. At round 5, findingStates keeps ref=R1 with reopened=R4, and the round-2 closure is not selected. findingObligations gives F1 the round-1 origin, which has no regression paths, so a still-present F1 in round 5 is demoted and the pair can PASS. claimRecords would likewise take the classId of the old F1.","id":"CR-2","path":"repository/plugin-src/shared/resources/protocol/current/execution.js","severity":"low"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"affected-suite-receipt-missing","classStatement":"An affected test suite (desktop) whose staged code the candidate changes has no local receipt on this tree.","criterion":"gate:review","evidence":"The consumed runner receipts (evidence/5ce44ca4…) contain only CLI test files, plugin validate and `diff -r plugin-src/shared/resources/protocol apps/desktop/resources/protocol`; `npm test --prefix apps/desktop` was not run. Mitigations: the mirror is byte-identical (diff exit 0). stage-protocol.test.js:17-25 compares only listed bytes. apps/desktop main/renderer/preload/test do not use claims. ciRequiredChecks includes 'Desktop contract on Ubuntu 24.04' (plugin-ci.yml:142-160, `npm run check` = desktop `npm test`).","failureScenario":"The candidate restages apps/desktop/resources/protocol (adds current/sweep-records.js, changes execution.js, boundary-review.js and review-findings.js) and edits desktop-projection.json. If the staged projection or a desktop protocol test broke, the problem would only show in hosted Desktop contract CI after convergence, as a visible, recoverable failure.","id":"CR-3","path":"repository/apps/desktop/resources/desktop-projection.json","severity":"low"}],"gate":"review","historyAssessment":null,"mandateAssessment":{"evidence":"Goal: close #144 (AC4 sweep records) including cross-round regression tracking. Implemented: sweep-records.js, the packet records and the swept-class regression in boundary-review.js roundSchedule. #134 was folded in and recorded as DEC-4/S5 with its rationale (same function and class), as the constraint asks. Agent-answered decisions D1-D7, S2-S9 and DEC-2..5 are recorded with alternatives and reversibility. None contradicts the mandate or makes an irreversible human-level choice: the required `disposition` is a reversible input-contract change called out in plan P7. Edits to execution.js and boundary-review.js are limited to the import and checkClaims call, the packet fields and revision, one instruction sentence, the reuse key, the 3-line re-report change and roundSchedule sweeps; local-agent-adapters.js has no related change. The record format is versioned 'sweep-record/1' for #148 extensions. No merge evidence; docs use generic example paths. The PR description is not yet available to assess, which is a caveat rather than a violation.","outcome":"PASS"},"outcome":"FAIL","summary":"Feature review of tb-convergence-sweep-records at head 850684dd (base and target 6874b9d6). The sweep-record contract, the packet records, the swept-class regression rule, the reviewer guidance, the docs and the #134 cases are implemented, and every rubric criterion R1-R7 is tested. The implementer's verification receipts show 52/52 focused tests passing, plus the affected suites, plugin validation and the mirror diff. One medium, blocking defect remains, introduced by the #134 change in review-findings.js. The peer prepass now counts every finding a report raises as 'present in its round', not only re-reports of earlier IDs. Rounds are now grouped by (scope, round, revision), and a re-review of an unchanged revision keeps the same round. Together, these mean a finding first raised on a revision can never be closed by a later `unsupported` or `resolved` on that same revision. That breaks the disputed-claim re-review flow in later rounds: the finding stays open and unreconciled even though both reviewers accepted the rebuttal. There are two non-blocking low findings: (1) the execution.js re-report change keeps the oldest raising report as origin when a finding ID is reused; (2) no local receipt for the desktop test suite (planned hosted CI covers it). Caveats: no authoritative changed-path list was supplied, so changed paths were found from content and base comparison. The planning documents are outside the repository snapshot. PR text is not yet available."}]}